        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Choose opacity first, then a color family that suits the house and the wood. For most decks, a semi-transparent cedar or light brown is the practical choice: it hides dirt, flatters softwoods, stays cooler than dark colors and is easy to maintain. Use grays and very dark tones deliberately, usually in semi-solid or solid versions and often on railings rather than floors.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then test. Buy samples, apply them to cleaned, brightened boards on your own deck, let them dry and look at them in different light next to the house. Start a little lighter if you are unsure, since going darker later is easy and going lighter is not.
          </p>
        </div>

